=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: switch0
diagnosis_text:
The IT Department outage is not caused by router0 — router0 is completely
healthy. All three of its interfaces (eth0 192.168.1.1/24, eth1
192.168.2.1/24, eth2 10.10.0.1/8) are UP with correct addresses, FRRd is
running, and RIP routes to every remote subnet are installed in the FIB. The
blame lies entirely with switch0.

switch0 operates as a Linux bridge (br0) with eth0 uplinked to router0 and
eth1 serving lan_it (the IT Department LAN). Both physical interfaces are UP
and correctly enslaved to br0, but the bridge itself is in DOWN state
(confirmed by `ip link show br0`: flags `<BROADCAST,MULTICAST>` with no UP
flag, state DOWN). With the bridge down, the Linux kernel does not forward any
frames between the two ports; additionally, because STP is enabled on this
bridge, both ports are reported as STP `disabled` — they cannot transition to
forwarding while the bridge device is down. The net result is a complete Layer
2 black hole between the IT LAN and router0's eth0.

IT hosts can still reach one another because they share the lan_it collision
domain directly and do not traverse the switch0 bridge for intra-subnet
traffic. Every packet that must cross to router0 (gateway-bound or
off-subnet) is silently dropped at switch0. Router0's ARP table contains no
192.168.1.x entries, confirming no frame has reached it from lan_it.

The startup script on switch0 correctly includes `ip link set dev br0 up`, so
the bridge was operational at boot; the fault was introduced post-startup by
bringing br0 down (e.g., `ip link set br0 down`).

Root cause: switch0's bridge interface br0 was brought down after startup,
severing the L2 path between the IT Department LAN and its gateway on router0.

Corrective fix: on switch0, run `ip link set br0 up`. The bridge ports will
transition through STP and reach forwarding state within a few seconds,
restoring full IT Department connectivity.
=== END ===
